          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            While fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is at some level well described by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            used to define such a QFT can reasonably be expected to define only degrees of freedom and interactions that are relevant up to some given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            scale. In this perspective one speaks of the theory as being the
            %em effective quantum field theory
            of some – possibly known but possibly unspecified – more fundamental theory.
          %p
            An example (historically the first to be successfully considered) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermi+theory+of+beta+decay"} Fermi theory of beta decay
            of hadrons: this contains interactions of four
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutron"} neutron
            decays into a collection consisting of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proton"} proton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            and a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrino"} neutrino
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-boson"} W-boson
            %em effective
            theory made very precise predictions at energy scales less than 10
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MeV"} MeV
            = succeed "-boson" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GeV"} GeV
            %em effectively
            invisible at these low energies.
          %p
            The low energy expansion of any unitary, relativistic,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossing+symmetry"} crossing symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            can be described by an effective quantum field theory.
          %p
            In the perspective of effective field theory notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalizable+interaction"} non-renormalizable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            Lagrangians can still make perfect sense as effective theories and give rise to well defined predictions: they can be effective approximations to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalizable+interaction"} renormalizable
            more fundamental theories. This is sometimes called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+completion"} UV completion
            of the given effective theory.
          %p
            For instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            – which is notoriously
            %span.newWikiWord
              non-renormalizable
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/non-renormalizable+interaction"} ?
            – makes perfect sense as an effective field theory (see for instance the introduction in (
            %a{:href => "#DonoghueIntroduction"}> Donoghue
            ). It is in principle possible that there is some more fundamental theory with plenty of excitations at high energies that is however degreewise finite in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %em effective
            description at low energy is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalizable+interaction"} non-renormalizable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is meant to be such a theory.)
          %h2#details Details
          %p
            The concept of effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            has a precise formulation in the rigoruous context of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InCausalPerturbationTheory"} Effective pQFT in Causal perturbation theory
          %p
            Effective quantum field theory has traditioanlly been discussed informally, referring to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            intuition:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TraditionalInformalDiscussion"}> Traditional informal arguments
                \-
          %h3#InCausalPerturbationTheory In causal perturbation theory
          %p
            We discuss the rigorous formulation of effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            in terms of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BrunettiDuetschFredenhagen09"} Brunetti-Dütsch-Fredenhagen 09, section 5.2
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Duetsch10"} Dütsch 10
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Duetsch18"} Dütsch 18, section 3.8
          %h4#UVRegularization (“Re”-)Normalization via UV-Regularization
          #CutoffsUVForPerturbativeQFT.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoffs"}> UV cutoffs
                )
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_{\text{BV-BRST}}, \mathbf{L}', \Delta_H )
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              over
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              (according to
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "S-matrix#VacuumFree"} this def.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_H = \tfrac{i}{2}(\Delta_+ - \Delta_-) + H
              is the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
              inducing the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi F
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Γ
                    %msub
                      %mo ′
                      %mrow
                        %mi Σ
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo ⊠
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mtext BV-BRST
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta_F \in \Gamma'_{\Sigma \times \Sigma}(E_{\text{BV-BRST}} \boxtimes E_{\text{BV-BRST}})
            %p
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi F
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mi i
                          %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo +
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_F = \tfrac{i}{2}(\Delta_+ + \Delta_-) + H
            %p
              Then a choice of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+cutoffs"} UV cutoffs
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
              around this vacuum is a collection of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-singular+distributions"} non-singular distributions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{F,\Lambda}
              parameterized by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+real+numbers"} positive real numbers
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
