                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h3#TraditionalInformalDiscussion Traditional informal discussion
          %p Traditional informal discussion of effective field theory proceeds from the following claim
          %p
            %em
              For a given set of asymptotic states,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
              with the most general
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
              containing all terms allowed by the assumed symmetries will yield the most general
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              elements consistent with analyticity,
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  perturbative unitarity
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/perturbative+unitarity"} ?
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cluster+decomposition"} cluster decomposition
              and the assumed symmetries.
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Weinberg79"}> Weinberg 1979
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#Leutwyler94"}> Leutwyler94
            ); reviewed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Pich"} Pich, p. 6
          %p Based on this, one argues to obtains an effective approximation to a given more fundamental theory (which may or may not be actually known) by
          %ol
            %li
              %p choosing the (sub)set of fields to be considered;
            %li
              %p
                writing down a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi eff
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi O
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      L_{eff} = \sum_i c_i O_i
              %p
                that contains
                %em all
                the possible polynomial interaction terms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi O
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_i
                of these fields scaled by their expected/known energy scale
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi O
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [O_i] = d_i
              %p (this will in general contain lots of direct interaction that in the fundamental theory are really compound interactions)
              %p
                with
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∝
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi Λ
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi d
                                  %mi i
                                %mo −
                                %mi dim
                                %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_i \propto \frac{1}{\Lambda^{d_i - dim X}}
            %li
              %p finally one fixes all the coupling constants of all these interactions by
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    either deriving them from a known fundamental theory by
                    %em integrating out
                    higher energy effects in that theory;
                %li
                  %p or, otherwise, measuring them in the laboratory. The point being that due to the energy cutoff, this is guaranteed to be a finite number of parameters. After these have been determined, all remaining quantities given by the Lagrangian are then predictions of the effective theory.
